[%- END %]
[%- IF html %]</code></pre>[% ELSE %]
[% END -%]

[% IF html %]<p>[% END -%]
Frankly, this isn't exactly the same as accessing through
status.rapidcow.org [% WRAPPER $smul %]with[% END %] the domain name (I believe
somewhere in the SSL handshake the client sends the domain
name so the server would return the correct certificate[% dash -%]
[% UNLESS html %]
[% END %]which was the reason I was able to host multiple domains yet
have separate SSLs sent back for each), but if we assume the
underlying HTTP server just looks at the Host header in the
HTTP request I send (judging by my other site hosted on
Codeberg Pages this seems to be the case), suffice it to say
that I have reliably produced what would have happened [% WRAPPER $smul %]if[% END %]
I switched back to Codeberg now.  Plus playing with the
DNS record just for this experiment would be too silly, so
reasonably I decided not to[% ldots %]   but instead play this
trick to get around the mismatching SSL certificate name
(FYI, subdomain certificates with a CN entry like what I got
above with "CN=*.codeberg.page" are only one-level deep,
so an Internet-standard abiding client would deem it invalid
[%- dash1 %]see third section of RFC2818 for reference.  On the other
hand, s_client is just an SSL client, so it doesn't validate
that consistency, and I can still send an HTTP request in
spite of all that. :)
[%- IF html %]</p>[% ELSE %]
[% END -%]

[% IF html %]<p>[% END -%]
So that is the state of Codeberg Pages now[% dots %] I couldn't
get it to work back when I still ran on it, and I don't
think I will. :(  But maybe stepping away is a good thing:
Codeberg Pages is <a href="https://codeberg.org/Codeberg/pages-server/issues/399">maintained by one person</a>, I feel that I
shouldn't asking them to look into this minute problem[% dots %]
[%- IF html %]</p>[% ELSE %]
[% END -%]

[% IF html %]<p>[% END -%]
Then well, I don't know how many of you noticed that somewhat
unsettling red-text at the bottom of the page[% dash %]well for
a while (a few weeks up until about a day ago) I hosted this
on the same server where I put the funny-looking maintenance
pages for rapidcow.org and friends, and also that initial
version of rapidcow.org with the home page full with GIF[% dots %]
you know which server I am talking about.  It's the one I
rented from Digital Ocean since like February. 164.90.136.30
[%- dash1 %]you might recognize it from that IPv4 address, even.
(If not, try to [% WRAPPER $code %]<a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dig_(command)">dig</a> A rapidcow.org[% END %] sometime later ;)
[%- IF html %]</p>[% ELSE %]
[% END -%]

[% IF html %]<p>[% END -%]
It was quite frantic when I migrated[% dash %]I archived the nginx
logs, had to remove the existing nginx configs (because the
maintenance pages would go up again and I don't want that
to happen), then used [% WRAPPER $code %]rsync(1)[% END %] to push the files, created a
server config that simply served the files using [% WRAPPER $code %]<a href="http://nginx.org/en/docs/http/ngx_http_core_module.html#try_files">try_files</a>[% END %];
then I swapped out the CNAME record for an A record, waited
for it to propogate, ran certbot to issue SSL certificates
for my status subdomain, then finally tweaked the nginx config
slightly to my liking (I keep separate logs for port 80 (HTTP)
and port 443 (HTTPS) for no reason[% dots %] right :/)
[%- IF html %]</p>[% ELSE %]
[% END -%]

[% IF html %]<p>[% END -%]
But everything was fine! except people (or I guess bots)
[% WRAPPER $smul %]immediately[% END %] started to access my other sites like
rapidcow.org and www.yizx.net whose DNS records still
pointed to this server!  I don't know if anyone got
spooked by the bad Host header error I let nginx send back[% dots %]
but that's what I made nginx do when no config matches.
[%- # I once posted about it - I have zero idea where it is now. # %]
[%- # (Right, i should be deleting my cringe mastodon posts....) # %]
Still I thought it could be confusing to get a 4xx error
instead of a 5xx one, so instead of being the responsible
sysadmin to configure new nginx server blocks to do just that
I removed the DNS records for my domains so that people
wouldn't make the connection in the first place[% dots %] (to this
day if you run [% WRAPPER $code %]dig A rapidcow.org[% END %] you would get nothing,
which is[% dots %] kind of sad, and I do apologize for that :c)
[%- IF html %]</p>[% ELSE %]
[% END -%]

[% IF html %]<p>[% END -%]
But back to the server.  Something went morbidly wrong when
I tried to install ubuntu-desktop on the server last night
(I wanted to see how this "X11 forwarding" thing I've heard
worked over SSH[% dots %] for no good reason u_u):  The server
stopped connecting to the Internet.  I couldn't install or
update with [% WRAPPER $code %]apt-get[% END %], I couldn't [% WRAPPER $code %]ping(1)[% END %] any domain (heck
they wouldn't even resolve in the first place)[% dash %]but worst
of all[% dots %]  I couldn't log into it from my laptop via SSH.
This meant that in the past few hours I had been using the
Web console provided by Digital Ocean (the so-called "Rescue
console", but that's only because the other console hang
since the server was basically offline and the rescue console
is the console that interacts with the server on the private
network locally).
[%- IF html %]</p>[% ELSE %]
[% END -%]

[% SET en0 = 'https://www.digitalocean.com/community/questions/no-internet-connection-after-droplet-reboot' -%]
[% IF html %]<p>[% END -%]
Now, know that I'm not saying that the server files got
corrupted.  It was purely a network problem which [% WRAPPER $smul %]may[% END %] have
been caused by ubuntu-desktop (or the x2goserver I installed
shortly after) was bringing its own network manager that was
<a href="[% en0 %]">conflicting with the then network manager</a>.
The solution was almost trivial though: to get the Internet
to work I only had to hook the eth0 interface to its gateway
and subnet mask, like this:
[%- IF html %]</p>[% ELSE %]
[% END -%]

[% IF html %]<pre><code>[% END %]
[%- FILTER html -%]
ifconfig eth0 <ipv4 address> netmask <subnet mask> up
ip route add default via <gateway address>
[%- END %]
[%- IF html %]</code></pre>[% ELSE %]
[% END -%]

[% IF html %]<p>[% END -%]
The IPv4 address here is indeed the public 164.90.136.30
that you already know.  These are commands were all it took
to bring my server back on the Internet.  And I did not have
to edit /etc/resolv.conf to use Google's DNS server (8.8.8.8)
because Digital Ocean's DNS servers work just fine (plus, not
a lot of posts I've seen mention this, but chances are you're
not supposed to edit /etc/resolv.conf directly?[% dots %] mine was a
symlink to a file deeply nestled inside /run/ and had a huge
block of comment at the top telling me to not edit it.)
But I just couldn't for the life of me figure out how to
tell it to configure those [% WRAPPER $smul %]on boot[% END %][% dash %]as soon as I reboot
all of that had to be re-run; and since SSH is busted without
Internet connection, all of this could only be re-run on the
rescue console[% dots %] which makes things pretty annoying.
[%- FILTER html %] >:([% END %][% IF html %]</p>[% ELSE %]
[% END -%]

[% IF html %]<p>[% END -%]
I tried <a href="https://docs.digitalocean.com/support/how-do-i-debug-my-droplets-network-configuration/">
[%- WRAPPER $code %]netplan try --debug[% END %]</a> and <a href="https://serverfault.com/a/968142">
[%- WRAPPER $code %]netplan apply --debug[% END %]</a>,
and all it did was disconnect my SSH connection and never
restore the IP stuff.  I tried to install and re-install the
[% WRAPPER $code %]cloud-init[% END %] package and run <a href="https://www.digitalocean.com/community/questions/eth0-not-getting-configured-correctly-after-upgrade">
[%- WRAPPER $code %]dpkg-reconfigure cloud-init[% END %]</a>,
checked /etc/netplan/50-cloud-init.yaml, loaded and quite
possibly re-generated it with netplan, [% WRAPPER $code %]apt remove --purge[% END %]d
and reinstalled [% WRAPPER $code %]cloud-init[% END %], [% WRAPPER $code %]landscape-common[% END -%], and
[% WRAPPER $code %]network-manager[% END %] (since one of the complaints in
/var/log/cloud-init.log was that it couldn't find
NetworkManager, so I figured it could be a dependency)[% dash2 -%]
it's no use.  Once I reboot I end up right where I started.
[%- IF html %]</p>[% ELSE %]
[% END -%]

[% IF html %]<p>[% END -%]
Now I could continue trying by opening a support ticket on
Digital Ocean, but I was tired, and it's almost new year
(not like people work on new year), and things don't matter
[% WRAPPER $smul %]that[% END %] much[% dots %] so after taking out the latest nginx logs that
had accumulated primarily before this disaster struck (the
thing is as I said before, once the port becomes operational
the logs are [% WRAPPER $smul %]instantaneous[% END %][% dash %]even if I finish typing in
those two commands, there will be SSH login attempts, there
will be HTTP requests made directly to the IP address with
no Host header (likely from port scanning bots)[% dots %] so a
minority of logs would come after, interestingly enough)
I reinstalled the whole OS.  And phew it felt refreshing to
see 80% of the disk space being free again[% dots %] don't have to
go around hunting for unused files (like I would have to for
a system that I am still actively using, sigh[% dots %] this feels
good, honestly =v= and it's now automatically upgraded to
the latest LTE of Ubuntu too!)
[%- IF html %]</p>[% ELSE %]
[% END -%]

[% IF html %]<p>[% END -%]
Of course nothing is configured yet and there shouldn't be
any service running on any ports (except for sshd on port 22
because obviously I have to log in)[% dots %] as to what this
status page is now hosted by, well it is SourceHut!
I know it sounds a lot like SourceForge, but SourceHut[% dots %]
to me it's like GitHub but with less clutter.  Closer to
Gitweb and the <a title="Seriously, these guys don't get paid enough" href="https://www.reddit.com/r/git/comments/apbbt/repoorcz_sponsor_pulls_out_needs_pledges_for_new/">humble</a> repo.or.cz (and <a href="https://git.zx2c4.com/cgit/about/">cgit</a>, what I once used
for git.rapidcow.org), though the design looks nicer and
cleaner, and it runs services that go beyond (though heavily
revolve around) Git.  <a href="https://srht.site/">SourceHut Pages</a> is one such service
that I really like because once you build and install this
<a href="https://sr.ht/~xenrox/hut/">hut</a> command guy all you have to do to get a web site up is
upload a tarball and specify a domain name and[% dots %] that's it.
Then you point your DNS record to srht.site and it just works:
[%- IF html %]</p>[% ELSE %]
[% END -%]

